  "query_preview": "..\n\nKey Message 1)\n\n\nA mid level shortwave will rotate thru the base of a long wave trof\nover southern Canada and the Great Lakes. A surface cold front\nbisecting ILN/s area will push southeast through the region \novernight. Northwest to north flow in the wake of this front will \nprovide a reinforcement of seasonably cool and dry air, which will \nsettle in through the first part of the week.\n\nHigh temperatures look to top out in the upper 60s to the lower 70s \ntoday or about 3 to 5 degrees below normal. High pressure of Canadian\norigin will build southeast into the area tonight. Clear skies and \ncalm winds will create favorable conditions for some patchy frost to\ndevelop. This will be mainly across the northern counties in \nsheltered/low- lying areas. Have continued to adjust NBM temperatures \na little cooler with lows from the upper 30s northeast to the lower\nand middle 40s southwest. Some very patchy frost cannot be ruled out\nagain Tuesday night into Wednesday, mainly for low-lying/sheltered \nareas of central/south central Ohio and northeast Kentucky. \n\nWarmer air will slowly build back into the Ohio Valley mid to late\nweek, with highs reaching into the upper 70s to lower 80s by \nWednesday through the remainder of the week. \n\nKey Message 2) \n\nA developing low initially over the Gulf will progress to the north-\nnortheast into the Ohio and Tennessee Valleys next weekend. This will\nbring the next chance for rain next Sunday. There is model spread so\nuncertainty is moderate to high regarding placement and timing of the\npotential rain. Expect dry period of weather until this potential\nfor rain exists for the second half of next weekend.",
